WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.411
"HMB, Magnesium, Omega-3, and Vitamin D Supplements
for Age-Related Muscle Loss"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.040
Previously,
I've covered the evidence

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.320
surrounding supplementation
for older adults with protein

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.520
or amino acids like leucine.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.680
What about a metabolite
of leucine

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.360
known as beta-hydroxy-
beta-methylbutyrate,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.560
known as HMB?

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.000
Adding HMB
to an exercise regimen

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.560
fails across the board

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.360
to improve muscle mass,
muscle strength,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.080
or physical performance
among older individuals,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.120
and since everyone
should be exercising,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.440
that pretty much
takes HMB off the table.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.440
But there are circumstances
when you're laid up,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.640
for example, recuperating
from a broken hip.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.320
Researchers in Spain randomized

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.480
about 100 men and women
over the age of 65 in rehab

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.840
recovering from a hip fracture
to a nutritional supplement

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.040
with about 3 grams of HMB.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.840
They suffered less loss
of fat-free mass

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.920
compared to the control group
who didn't get the supplement.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.400
Unfortunately, the supplement
provided other components,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.440
so it's impossible
to tease out the HMB effects,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.800
but regardless,
there was no significant change

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.480
in muscle strength
or muscle function.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.560
This was roughly consistent
with a 10-day bedrest study

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.200
of straight HMB versus placebo

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.720
in which the HMB group
similarly lost less lean mass-

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.360
but only when an outlier
was excluded,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.040
which isn't really kosher-

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.600
and there was
no apparent improvement

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.160
in strength or function.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.520
This is what meta-analyses
of all such studies have found-

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.240
an improvement in fat-free mass

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.920
in older sedentary adults

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.480
with no change
in strength or function

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.000
and no benefits
for any muscle parameters

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.560
when combined
with physical activity.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.280
Since we all should be
exercising,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.440
and there's a questionable
benefit of lean mass

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.720
for lean mass's sake,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.720
combined with the fact
that HMB activates

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.480
the engine-of-aging
enzyme mTOR,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.400
I see little reason
to recommend

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.040
supplementing with HMB.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.280
What about magnesium?

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.320
Higher blood levels correlate
with muscle performance.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.240
And one cohort study
of older adults

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.200
found that those
with higher magnesium intake

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.600
had greater lean mass
over time,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.480
though the dietary intake
of magnesium

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.360
appeared to be similar
between those

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.520
with and without sarcopenia,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.200
excessive age-related
muscle loss.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.720
There was one
randomized controlled trial

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.400
of magnesium supplements

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.640
that found that those taking
about the RDA for 12 weeks

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.520
had no effect on lean mass

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.640
or upper or lower body strength,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.720
but it did appear
to improve walking speed

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.920
and chair to stand times.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:35.680
Unfortunately, it wasn't

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.320
a randomized
placebo-controlled trial,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.320
so that may all have just been
the placebo effect.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.320
What about omega-3
fatty acid supplements?

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.920
Some population studies suggest

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.360
that fish consumption
is associated

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.880
with greater muscle strength.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.080
In others, seafood consumption
was associated

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.000
with a greater loss
of muscle strength over time,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.680
maybe because of
its mercury content.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.480
When put to the test
in placebo-controlled trials,

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.000
fish oil and other
omega-3 supplements

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.280
showed no effect on lean mass,
or muscle strength,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.840
or clinically significant
effects

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.320
on functional performance.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.880
What about vitamin D?

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.240
Osteomalacia and rickets,
softened bone diseases

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.800
resulting from severe
vitamin D deficiency,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.680
are often accompanied
by muscle weakness.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.800
Mice genetically engineered
without vitamin D receptors

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.760
suffer muscle atrophy,

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.000
and vitamin D-depleted rats
show impaired muscle function.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.400
In a Petri dish, vitamin D
increased muscle fiber growth.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.040
Human observational studies
show correlations

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.640
between lower
vitamin D blood levels

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.840
and lower muscle mass,
lower muscle strength,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.920
and performance
such as walking speed,

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.080
and higher odds of prefrailty
or frailty.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.680
Of course, rather than
low vitamin D levels

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.080
leading to frailty,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.280
frailty may be leading
to low levels

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.120
of the sunshine vitamin,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.520
because frail individuals
may not be out

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.160
walking around as much.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.520
You don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.920
Multiple meta-analyses
of randomized controlled trials

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.400
found that giving
vitamin D supplements to people

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.840
with low vitamin D levels

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.720
significantly improved
their muscle strength

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.560
and/or performance-

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.000
that is, until multiple studies
were retracted

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.880
for fabricated data.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.560
It turns out, if you exclude
the fraudulent studies,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.240
the benefit evaporates.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.960
If anything, there may be
small adverse effects

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.560
to vitamin D supplementation
on muscle health.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.040
The 180-degree turn
demonstrates

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.440
that the "publication
of fake data

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:57.160
can have
major health implications."

